mates briefly.  All auto-attacks made against the garou have a +1 penalty
to land a successful hit.  Additionally, while active, Lambent Flame casts
light into the room you are in.
~
0 chiropteranmarauder~

{RChiropteran Marauder{x
{R       Vicissitude {W******{x

An even more terrifying version of Awaken the Zulo Shape, this power
transforms the vampire into a terrifying, bipedal bat, bearing a wickedly
fanged maw and veined, leathery wings.  This power confers all of the
benefits of the zulo shape, in addition to a few others.  The mere sight of
the marauder is enough to make mortals or weak-willed vampires flee in
horror.  
 
{WTable Top / Role Play{x

{WSystem:  {xThe vampire gains all the effects of the zulo shape.  In addition,
the fluted wings allow flight at 25 mph, during which the vampire may carry,
but not manipulate, objects of reasonable size.  The player may make a
Strength + Body Crafts roll (difficulty 6) to extend bony claws at the end
of the wings, where hands should be.  These claws inflict Strength + 2 levels
of aggravated damage.  Also, the vampire subtracts two from the difficulty of
all hearing-based Perception rolls (though he adds one to vision-based Wits
and Perception rolls).  Assuming the mantle of the Chiropteran Marauder costs
three blood points.
 
{WHack and Slash / Out of Character:{x
 
System:  Character spends 80 blood points to assume the Chiropteran Marauder
form.  You gain the same benefits as zulo form, with the addition of flight.
Additionally, you may spend 10 blood points to create bony claws on your
wings.  These claws give a + 2 damage dice bonus to your unarmed attacks.

 
~
102 ifchecks checks~

The following are all of the current checks that can be made in progs.
$n is the char that triggered the program. $i is the mob running the
program. Any valid $ code can be substituted. See '{yhelp wildcards{x'
for valid $ wildcards.
{x
if rand 30              - if random number < 30 
if mobhere fido         - is there a 'fido' here 
if objhere bottle       - is there a 'bottle' here 
if mobhere 1233         - is there mob vnum 1233 here 
if objhere 1233         - is there obj vnum 1233 here 
if mobexists fido       - is there a fido somewhere 
if objexists sword      - is there a sword somewhere 
if people > 4           - does room contain > 4 people 
if players > 1          - does room contain > 1 pcs 
if mobs > 2             - does room contain > 2 mobiles 
if clones > 3           - are there > 3 mobs of same vnum here 
if order == 0           - is mob the first in room 
if hour > 11            - is the time > 11 o'clock 
if ispc $n              - is $n a pc 
if isnpc $n             - is $n a mobile 
if isgood $n            - is $n good 
if isevil $n            - is $n evil 
if isneutral $n         - is $n neutral 
if isimmort $n          - is $n immortal 
if ischarm $n           - is $n charmed 
if isfollow $n          - is $n following someone 
if isactive $n          - is $n's position > SLEEPING 
if isdelay $i           - does $i have mobprog pending 
if isvisible $n         - can mob see $n 
if hastarget $i         - does $i have a valid target 
if istarget $n          - is $n mob's target 
if exists $n            - does $n exist somewhere 
if affected $n blind    - is $n affected by blind 
if act $i sentinel      - is $i flagged sentinel 
if off $i berserk       - is $i flagged berserk 
if imm $i fire          - is $i immune to fire 
if carries $n sword     - does $n have a 'sword' 
if carries $n 1233      - does $n have obj vnum 1233 
if wears $n lantern     - is $n wearing a 'lantern' 
if wears $n 1233        - is $n wearing obj vnum 1233 
if has $n weapon        - does $n have obj of type weapon 
if uses $n armor        - is $n wearing obj of type armor 
if name $n puff         - is $n's name 'puff' 
if pos $n standing      - is $n standing 
if clan $n 'whatever'   - does $n belong to clan 'whatever' 
if race $n dragon       - is $n of 'dragon' race 
if class $n mage        - is $n's class 'mage' 
if objtype $p scroll    - is $p a scroll 
if vnum $i == 1233      - virtual number check 
if hpcnt $i > 30        - hit point percent check 
if room $i == 1233      - room virtual number 
if sex $i == 0          - sex check 
if level $n < 5         - level check 
if align $n < -1000     - alignment check 
if money $n > 2000      - Money check (total, gold + silver)
if objval0 > 1000       - object value[] checks 0..4 
if grpsize $n > 6       - group size check 
if quest_step $n == 100 - quest status check 
if remort $n >= 50      - check remorts on char
~
102 conditionals ifstatements orstatement andstatement~

In MUD programs, like a regular program, you can use conditionals to execute
sections of code only if certain conditions are met. If statements allow you
to add variety and real autonomy to a program. Programs can be aware of their
environment and react accordingly.
{x
Examples:
Fernando is a City Guard MOB at the city gates.
He greets every person entering the city.
{x
Code:
{cmob echoat $n Fernando says, "Welcome to City."
{cif uses $n weapon
{c    mob echoat $n Fernando looks at you and scowls.
{c    mob echoat $n He says, "Please put your weapons away. We are a peaceful city."
{celse
{c    mob echoat $n Fernando smiles at you, "Please enjoy your stay."
{cendif
{x
This program echos a greeting at every player that enters the room. It then
checks if the player is using an item type "weapon", and if they are, Fernando
asks them to put their weapons away. If not, he echos something pleasant.
{x
You can also use and/or to make your conditions more specific.
{x
{cif ispc $n
{cand isimmort $n
{cand name $n 'Matthew'
{cand objhere cake
{cand hour > 20
{c    mob echoat $n You're not hungry, you're bored, get out of the kitchen!
{cendif
{x
{cif affected $n blind
{cor affected $n fireblind
{c    mob echoat $n You can't see me!
{cendif
{x
You can also nest conditionals within other conditionals.
{x
{cif hour == 12
{c    if players > 2
{c        mob echo some random echo.
{c    else
{c        mob echo less than 2 players
{c    endif
{celse
{c    echo it isn't noon.
{cendif
{x
Every 'if' needs an 'endif'. The 'and', 'or', and 'else' statements cannot be used
without an opening 'if' statement.
{x
See '{yhelp ifchecks{x' for a list of available checks that can be made.
~
0 donate~

Adventurers often come across a lot of loot in their travels.  Sometimes
items that were once needed become irrelevant, or one simply didn't need
the item in the first place, but it may be of use to another person.
 
Rather than selling off an item, an alternative is to leave the item in
the donation chest near Chester recall ({ce n e {C[from Sir Owen]{x).  You
can manually put the item yourself if you are in the room, or you can use
the {cdonate{x command.  {cDonate{x will immediately transfer the item from your
inventory directly into the donation chest.
 
{WSyntax:{c  donate <item>{x
 
There is currently no cost or limit to using the donate command.
~
0 combat kill fight~

For those who have played on a MUD before, combat on Haven will be 
quite familiar. The difference on Haven is that behind the scenes, 
it uses d10 based combat from the Storyteller System. For those 
unfamiliar with MUD combat, the following is a brief guide. (Note: 
Currently the d10 style combat system is only for Player characters; 
d10 combat for NPCs is under development.)
{x
{WInitiating Combat{x
There are a number of ways to initiate combat, the simplest being to 
simply type 'kill' then the name of your target. For example, 'kill 
peasant' or 'kill Henry'. There are also a number of skills that will 
initiate combat, such as backstab, kick, bash, etc. Once combat is 
initiated, it will proceed automatically with one 'round' of combat 
occurring approximately every 3 seconds. Each round includes all of 
the hits the player makes against their target, and all of the hits 
the target makes against the player.
{x
{WOne Hit{x
Each round is a collection of singular 'hits', each computed 
individually. Each hit follows the same basic routine. Dexterity and 
either Brawl (for unarmed) or Melee (for armed) are added together 
to form an attack roll. If this succeeds, the aggressor's Strength 
plus any modifiers are combined to form a Damage roll. The victim 
rolls soak and armor, which subtract from the damage successes. If 
any damage successes remain, they are multiplied by a modifier and 
applied as damage.
{x
{WResolution{x
When damage is taken, it is deducted from the character's Hit Points. 
If a character's Hit Points are reduced to zero, non-vampire 
characters die while vampire characters go into a state of torpor in 
which they must be beheaded to fully kill them. Once the target is 
killed, experience is calculated and awarded to each group member in 
the room.
~
