0 smell~

A vampire's sustenance comes from the lifeblood of living creatures,
as such the freshness of the blood is a large factor in repleneshing
your supply.  The quickest way to gain a sizeable portion of blood
into your system is to {cfeed{x from a recently slain enemy's corpse.
If you witnessed the kill (or were the one delivering the killing blow),
then there is no doubt about the freshness of the corpse.  However, if
you come across a corpse on your travels, the best way to determine if
it is safe to feed from is to {csmell{x it.  This will give you a rough
estimate of how long the corpse has been lying there, rotting.  Fresh
corpses are safe to feed from, while ones that have been festering
run the risk of poisoning oneself from feeding from a rancid corpse.
 
Syntax: {Csmell corpse{x
 
{RSee also:{W  Feed Vampire Bloodpool{x
~
0 fang fangs~

{RFangs{x
{W        ({RVampire{W){x

{WSyntax:  {cfangs{x

One of the biggest concerns of a vampire is keeping fed with a steady
supply of blood.  Vampires are uniquely equipped for this task with two
inch-long fangs that can be extended and withdrawn at will.  By extending
your fangs, it allows a vampire to feed in the middle of combat or upon a
fallen corpse.  By doing so, you can replenish your blood supply.  
 
This command will toggle your fangs in/out, and other players can plainly
see your fangs when extended.  To help keep track of whether you have your
fangs out or not, there is an option to set up on your prompt to show your
fang status.  See {yhelp prompt{x for details.
 
See also:  VAMPIRE FEED
~
0 ic icmode~

Haven of the Embraced is an RP-Encouraged MUD, and as such there is the
option to roleplay with other players.  Roleplaying with other players
provides an alternative way to earn experience points, as well as quest
points.  The longer you roleplay, the larger the rewards are.
 
In order to gain the benefits of roleplaying, all parties involved must
switch from Out Of Character {C({DOOC{C){x to In Character {W({CIC{W){x mode.  This is
quite simple, and the command to toggle (IC) mode on/off is {cicmode{x.  Be
advised that you are not allowed to sit around in (IC) mode when you are not
actively roleplaying with someone.  This is considered illegal, and if caught
idling in (IC) mode, your character is subject to being nuked.  Please
enjoy the benefits of roleplaying, but do not abuse the system.
 
You can see which players are currently (IC) by typing '{cwho{x', as it will
display ({CIn-Character{x) next to any players that are currently (IC).

For additional pointers while roleplaying, feel free to peruse the
help file for roleplay (rp).

See also: {WRoleplay{x
~
0 skillmap~

Every common skill on Haven can be linked back to your Character Sheet.
As you increase your character's abilities via freebies, you will learn
new skills to help you in your journey.  {CSkillmap{x gives a list of all
available skills in the MUD, and what Abilities are needed to acquire the
skills.  Once you increase your abilities to the appropriate level to learn
a skill, it will automatically be added to your list of known skills.  To
see a listing of your currently acquired skills, use the {Cskills{x and
{Cskill all{x.
~
0 prayers~

The supernatural beings that affect the world from the shadows are
not the only ones who have access to mystic abilities.  Secrets handed
down from generations of study in the natural world, to blessings from
one of any number of deities, can be unearthed by studious individual
minds who know where to look.  These powers, collectively known as
{CPrayers{x, find their root in a variety of sources.  The three
Character Sheet Knowledges that are associated with prayers are:
{yOccult{x, {yHearth Wisdom{x, and {yTheology{x.
 
Occult-  Ancient knowledge usually centered around the supernatural
Hearth Wisdom-  Common wisdom of the lower class, usually folklore
Theology-  Lore and mysteries centered around deities affecting the world
 
The commands associated with prayers are: {Cprayers{x, {Cprayer all{x, {Cpray{x.
Prayers will list all known prayers up to your level, while prayer all
will show every prayer you have learned, and what level you can access
each, regardless of your current level.  Prayers are additionally displayed
alongside skills using the {Cskills{x and {Cskill all{x commands.  Prayer will
display the mana cost of prayers, while skills will show your skill %.

To actually use one of your prayers, the command is {Cpray prayername <target>{x.
The full name is needed, for example the 'Create Water' prayer needs to be
used as such:  {Cpray 'create water' <target> {x.

The roll for all prayers is your Maximum Willpower.

Also help on:  OCCULT HEARTH_WISDOM THEOLOGY SKILLMAP
~
0 spell spells~

Haven of the Embraced does not have any traditional 'Spells' to
be found; rather it boasts a variety of different powers that tend
to mimic magical spells of myth and legend.  There are both Race-
Specific and General powers that can be learned throughout the game.
 
Vampires (and Ghouls) utilize the power of Blood to fuel Disciplines
to help themselves blend into society.  Werewolves employ mighty Gifts
bestowed upon them by Gaia to further their eternal fight against the
Wyrm.  Mages use their mystical understanding of Reality itself, bending
the world to their will with the use of tried and true Rotes.
 
In addition to Race-Specific powers, there are a handful of spell-like
abilities that can be learned by anyone who invests the proper points
on your character sheet.  These 'spells' take the form of 'Prayers' on
Haven, and typically originate from deties or natural forces.  Points
spent in Theology, Hearth Wisdom, and Occult will give you access to
a variety of powers.
 
Also help on:  DISCIPLINES GIFTS ROTES THEOLOGY HEARTH_WISDOM OCCULT
               PRAYERS
~
