0 action~

ACTION:  What to do with the dice
 
If you already know how dice rolls work ([HELP ROLL] if you don't), it's time 
to learn how to use them. For example, some actions are completely fairly 
immediately, such as ducking a punch or jumping a gap. Some, however, take a 
bit more time--building, researching, and other more gradual tasks such as 
climbing are all good examples. In this case, you make an extended action. An 
extended action is basically just a series of simple actions where you add your 
successes from each action together. For example, in order to fix your carriage 
you might need a total of 15 successes, so you would roll the dice until you 
had accumulated at least 15. The number of rolls it takes will usually tell you 
how long it takes you to finish...and if you botch at any point in the action, 
you'll have to deal with the consequences.
 
Sometimes you have to take an action against someone else, and that person 
isn't necessarily going to just sit there and let you do it. In that situation, 
you make a resisted action. In a resisted action you both roll your dice, and 
the person with the most successes wins. In some cases, you'll be taking an 
action that is both extended AND resisted, such as wrestling or a game of 
chess. In these situations you make a resisted action each turn, and the person 
with the most successes adds the addition successes they achieved to a 
tally--so someone who rolled 3 successes against his opponent's 1 success would 
add 2 successes to his tally. Whoever reaches a given number of successes in 
their tally wins the contest, so for a chess game one player might need to 
score 15 successes in their tally in order to win. 
 
Sometimes the number of successes you'll need will be given in the rules for 
whatever you want to do, and sometimes it will be up to you. In a standard 
table-top game the storyteller would set such a number, but since haven doesn't 
use a storyteller for roleplaying, in your case it will be easier just to agree 
on a number with the other player or players involved.  You are always welcome 
to request a storyteller to sit in onyour roleplays to help facilitate rolls.
 
See also Roll, Combat
~
0 roll dice~

ROLL: Chance, How-To, Botches and Successes
 
In Vampire: the Masquerade, dice are rolled differently than in many other 
games. Here, an individual die is much like a coin flip. To make a dice roll, 
first you determine how many dice you wish to roll, and a difficulty from 3-10. 
If an individual die result comes up greater than or equal to the difficulty 
number, it is a "success." If it is lower than the difficulty, it is a 
"failure."
 
For example, let's say you wanted to roll 3 dice with a difficulty of 6. You 
roll the dice and your first roll comes up as a 2, your second roll comes up as 
a 6, and your third roll comes up as a 7. The 2 counts as a failure, but the 6 
and the 7 are both successes, so you end up with 2 successes overall.
 
The difficulty of any given roll affects how likely it is to succeed. While 
Haven's dice roller can use any number for a difficulty, in actual play the 
difficulty will almost always be from 3-10. A difficulty of 6 gives you a 50-50 
chance of success or failure--basically, it's like flipping a coin.
 
3 - Easy: making a microwave dinner

4 - Routine: assembling do-it-yourself furniture

5 - Straightforward: provoking someone who's already cranky

6 - Standard: throwing a punch

7 - Challenging: taking an advanced math test

8 - Difficult: researching an ancient interpretive dance

9 - Extremely Difficult: performing magic rituals while break-dancing
 
A difficulty of 10 is possible, but extremely unlikely. If you find yourself 
rolling against difficulty 10, you are undertaking a task with very slim 
chances of success.
 
Most dice rolls you will use are going to have a difficulty of 6. In fact, if a 
difficulty is not given, you can safely assume that the difficulty of 6. Most 
of the time, you will roll against either difficulty 6 or, for a slightly more 
difficult task, difficulty 7. Other difficulties rarely occur unless something 
has somehow altered your chances of success.
 
So you've rolled your die with whatever difficulty was appropriate, and it came 
up as a success. Great! So what does that mean? Well, most rolls will involve 
more than one die, and the number of successes you get, if you get any, will 
determine just HOW successful you are. 
 
One success - Marginal: riding a bike without falling off

Two successes - Moderate: lopsided pancakes that still taste good

Three successes - Complete: getting an A on your math exam

Four successes - Exceptional: getting an A+ on that exam

Five successes - Phenomenal: redefining the art of pancakes entirely
 
No successes at all means you fail at your task normally. For instance, you 
failed to tune a piano, but at least you didn't break it.
 
Sometimes, though, you don't just fail at a task. Sometimes you REALLY mess it 
up, and so badly that you're even worse off than when you started. Whenever you 
roll a 1 on any roll, that 1 doesn't just count as a failure, it actually 
counts as a NEGATIVE success. That is to say, if you rolled 4, 6, 8, 1 against 
difficulty 6, you end up with one success total--the 4 was a failure, and the 6 
and the 8 were both successes, but the 1 actually canceled out with of those 
successes, so you end up with just one.
 
On top of that, sometimes you mess up so badly that the whole thing just blows 
up in your face. When you roll a 1 on any given dice roll and you don't roll 
any successes along with it, that's called a botch. If you rolled a 4 and a 1 
against difficulty 6, for example, that would be a botch. As long as there's 
one success, however, it's not a botch. Only when there are nosuccesses at all 
and at least one die comes up as a 1 is the roll a botch, and then disaster 
strikes.
 
A botch basically means that whatever could possibly have gone wrong...DID. If 
you were shooting a gun, it kicked back and hit you in the face. If you were 
driving a car, the brakes went out. If you were flirting with your crush, you 
farted loudly and woke up a sleeping bear in the woods.
 
To use the dice system type DICE
 
See also:  Action, Combat
~
