1 reveal sense~

{RHeightened Senses{x
     {RAuspex{x {W*{x
          
This power sharpens all of the vampire's senses, effectively doubling
the clarity and range of sight, hearing and smell.  While her sense of
taste and touch extend no farther than normal, they likewise become
far more acute.  The Kindred may magnify her senses at will, and can
sustain this power for as long as she desires.  Occasionally, this
talent provides extrasensory or even some precognitive insights.
These brief, unfocused glimpses may be flashes of empathy or eerie
feelings of foreboding.   Although, the vampire has no control over
such perceptions, with practice he can learn to interpret them with a
over such perceptions, with practice he can learn to interpret them with a fair
degree of accuracy.
                      
Expanded senses come at a price, however.  Bright lights, loud noises and strong 
smells present a hazard while the vampire uses this power.  In addition to the 
possibility for distraction mentioned above, an especially sudden stimulus, like 
the glare of a lantern or a clap of thunder, can blind or deafen the Kindred for
some time after.  The vampire may choose how many senses are heightened at any
given time, and many kindred judiciously use this power on the one sense that 
is needed for the task at hand. 
               
{WTable Top / Role Play{x
{WSystem:{x  No roll is normally required to activate, and this power is particularly
geared towards accentuating Role Playing.  In certain circumstances, however, the
use of this power will require a Roll.   
    
A vampire may Heighten his senses to bolster attempts at discovery.  This is 
is useful when the kindred is actively trying to detect hidden entrances,
stealthy thieves, or even an invisible mage.  Most frequently, when it is used
in this manner, a Canite will be trying to discover someone using Obfuscation.
The vampire may subtract his Auspex Rating from the following roll:
Perception + Alertness (Diff Varies)
          
The same roll and the lowered difficulty, afforded by this power, also apply
to the perception of threats, and immediate dangers, as the kindred, is more
keenly aware to his surroundings.  The extrasensory and precognative flashes
given by this power, are never under the willful control of the Canite, and
occur sporadically and randomly.  On very rare occasions, the supernatural
glimpse recieved from this power, will come at just the right moment, 
usually times of great turmoil, or when the vampire is in mortal danger.
     
This power does not let the vampire see in pitch darkness, but it does
reduce the difficulty penality by {R1,{x and the character my make ranged
attacks if they can see or otherwise detect their foe.   
       
{WHack and Slash / Out of Character{x
{WSyntax:  {creveal{x
While active, the Kindred gains the ability to detect hidden enemies (both mundane
and supernatural methods of hiding).  Additionally, she becomes vulnerable to sound
and light.
~
1 Hunger Thirst Hungry Thirsty Eat Drink Food~

{WHunger and Thirst{x{D:{x {WThe Eternal Struggle{x
      {D({xUnless You're A Vampire{D){x        
             
Hunger and thirst are very annoying MUD mechanics.  Haven is based upon the
ROM code base, and hunger and thirst came stock and have yet to be removed.
These mechanics were meant to add realism, as well as help drive a MUD's 
economy.  Food and drink, usually cost money, and this lead to more uses for
fountains, food items, and shops.  While a character is hungry or thirsty,
regeneration rates for hitpoints, mana, and movement, decline dramatically.
One of the most grating issues caused by hunger and thirst are the continuous
reminders that you are, in fact, starving and parched.  Lastly, food and drink
take up needed space in your inventory, which can be problematic at low levels.
But take heart, because Haven's code does not include, the ultimate suck ever,
version of hunger and thirst management.  Rejoice, for starvation and 
dehydration cannot kill you.     
                  
{WOut of Character / Hack and Slash{x
                  
Everyone on Haven becomes hungry or thirsty with two exceptions.  Immortals,
because, well, screw those blasted messages while you are are building, coding,
or writing help files, and then of course Vampires.  Mortals, Mages, and Garou
are still subject to dry mouth, and rumbling tummies.  
      
Food and drink can be found anywhere and everywhere in all manner of forms.
Shops fill the cities, hawking food such as eggs, turkish delight, and spam,
while, wineskins, chalices and flasks can be purchased for that endless
cottenmouth you seem to always have.  Fountains, streams, and pools of 
refreshment are prevelant for free refreshment, and the forests grow mushrooms,
berries, and vegetables, ripe for the picking.  Even the corpses of those slain
in combat, can be harvested, for that quick nosh on the adventure road. 
        
Not all foods and drinks are created equally however, as they all have
varied settings for how long they keep you full or satiated.  Some meals
are heavier than others, and some drinks more refreshing.  In addition, not 
everything that can be ingested should be.  Milk can go sour, bread moldy,
and meat rancid.  Some foods and drinks can poison you, but conversley some
are blessed with powerful magicks.  Alcohol is, ever present throughout the
game, and inbibing it, will subject you to the drunk code.  You have been
warned.  Finally, interactions with eating and drinking can sometimes be
problematic.  Filling up on liquids, will leave you unable to eat food at 
times, and being full will delay the use of pills and potions.  Of course
you could attempt to purge, but Vomit is just a social, and mechanically
does not empty your digestive track.
                                                        
The following commands and associated help topics, will provide you with all
the information needed in the eternal quest to remain fed and watered.
               
     
{WSyntax:{x Eat     {W<Food Name>{x
{WSyntax:{x Drink   {W<Source>{x
{WSyntax:{x Fill    {W<Container>   <Source>{x
{WSyntax:{x Invert  {W<Container>{x
{WSyntax:{x Get|Put {W<Food|Drink>  <Location>{x
{WSyntax:{x Buy     {W<Food|Drink>{x
              
The best has been saved for the very last.  The following command turns off the
annoying tick messages that you are already hungry again.  Use it and love it, 
but keep in mind, you will still grow hungry and thirsty, and these conditions 
will still apply the associated penalities if they are ignored. 
           
{W*{B*{G*{Y*{M*{R*{C*{x   {WSyntax:{x Nocondition   {C*{R*{M*{Y*{G*{B*{W*{x
    
{WSystem:{x Help {W<Topic>{x
                             
Help {WRegeneration{x        Help {WInventory{x
Help {WPoison{x              Help {WRegeneration{x
Help {WDrunk{x               Help {WPrayers{x
Help {WCondition{x           Help {WTick{x
~
