0 plead~

PLEAD:
{x
Syntax: plead <message>
{x
Plead sends a message to the Immortals and beeps to get their attention if they 
happen to be in another window. As we do not want players spamming Plead and 
beeping the Immortals incessantly, there is a small amount of lag associated 
with the command. This command should only be used if there are no Immortals 
visible, or if they are visible, AFK, and you need urgent attention. The feature 
can be selectively disabled and players that frequently abuse plead will have it 
turned off.
{x
See also help: channels, immortal, imms_list, rules
~
0 IMM_TITLES~

{x
IMM_TITLES:
Immortals on Haven can carry a couple of different titles based on the work
they do for the game. The following titles are currently in use and what those
Immortals do:
{x
{cAdministrator{w - Admins are the Immortals in charge of running the game.
{w      They handle issues between players, punishing gross offenses against
{w      policy, managing the other Immortals (assigning duties) as well as the
{w      day to day running of the MUD.
{x      
{bCoder{w - Coders are the Immortals that make actual changes to the features
{w      on Haven. They tweak old code, add new features, and work towards keeping
{w      the MUD fresh and exciting whether people play once a month or every day.
{x      
{rRoleplay{w - Roleplay/RP IMMs or Storytellers are the ones that assist players
{w      with IC issues. Resolving disputes about rolls, giving advice and guidance,
{w      and managing the Player-Killing process. Anything that involves the IC
{w      aspect of the game falls under the job of the Roleplay Immortals.
{x      
{gLaw/Enforcer{w - These Immortals are entrusted with keeping the peace and
{w      ensuring that all players follow the rules laid out in '{chelp rules{x'.
{w      Any accusations of cheating or harassment should go through a Law Immortal.
{w      Disagreements with Law Immortals can be resolved by appealing to an Admin.
{x      
{yBuilders{w - Builders are the unsung heroes of MUDs. None of the rooms, mobs,
{w      or objects in the game would exist without a builder having made them.
{w      They are in charge of adding new content in the form of areas for players
{w      to explore and mobprogs so mobs in the game interact with players in new
{w      ways.
{x    
{wAs the MUD grows, more titles may be added as more types of Immortals are
{wneeded to run the MUD smoothly.
{x
{wAlso see help: builder, immortal, rules, IC
~
1 hedit heditor help-editor~

{x
HEDIT
Syntax: edit help <keywd>  -Enters the editor for the selected helpfile.
Syntax: hedit <keyword>    -Enters the editor for the selected helpfile.
Syntax: hedit make <keywd> -Create a new helpfile and enters the editor.
{x
The follow commands are available from within the HEDITOR:
{x
commands            - prints a list of possible commands
make <keyword>      - Create a helpfile named <keyword> and opens for editing.
done                - exits the help editor and returns to normal play
show                - hitting return, or 'show' shows the help stats
keywords <keywords> - Changes the current helpfiles keywords to <string>
level <number>      - Changes the current helpfiles level to <number>
desc                - Enter the description editor for the current helpfile.
delete              - Marks the current helpfile for deletion.
{x
Note: Helps marked for deletion are not saved, but will remain on the MUD
until the MUD is rebooted and the helps read in again from the helps file.
{x
See also help: AEDIT, REDIT, MEDIT, OEDIT, MPEDIT
~
1 drawingoutthebeast~

{RDrawing Out The Beast{x
     {RAnimalism{x {W*****{x
                
At this level, the Kindred has keen understanding of the Beast Within. Whenever
this predator spirit threatens to overwhelm the vampire's soul and send him into
frenzy, he may instead release his feral urges upon another creature. The recipient
of the vampire's Beast is instantly overcome by frenzy.  This is an unnatural frenzy,
however, as the victim is channeling the Kindred's own fury.  As such, the vampire's
own behavior, expressions, and ever speech patterns are evident in the subject's
savage actions.  Gangrel and Tzimisce are especially fond of losing their Beasts on
others.  Gangrel do so to stir their ghouls into inspired heights of savagery during 
combat.  Tzimisce care less for who receives their Beast than they do for retaining
their own composure.
                   
{WTable Top / Role Play{x
                      
The Canite must be in frenzy or close to it to use this power, and the player must
announce his preferred target.  This must be someone within sight.
      
{WSystem:{x Manipulation + Animal Ken {WDiff:{x {R8{x
       
Refer to the following for the results:
   
{R1{x  Success    -   The Beast is unleashed on a random individual. 
{R2{x  Successes  -   The Beast is transfered successfully, but the  
                      vampire is stunned, and can't act the next turn.
{R3+{x Successes  -   The kindred transfers his Beast successfully.
            
If the attempt fails, the intensity of the frenzy actually increases.  As
the character relaxes in expectation of relieving his savage urges, the Beast 
takes that opportunity to dig deeper.  In this case, the frenzy lasts twice as 
long as normal, and is twice as difficult to shrug off, with the severity 
increasing exponentially.  Botching this roll is catastrophic.  The frenzy
grows so extreme that not even expending Willpower curbs its duration or 
effects.  The character is a hapless victim to the terrible fury of the Beast.
               
Even with success, this power can be dangerous.  If the kindred and victim are
seperated, before the frenzy is expended, the vampire cannot reclaim his Beast.
While no longer vulnerable to frenzy, the character cannot use or regain Will
power and becomes increasingly lethargic.  To recover the Beast, he must find
this poor soul.  The most effective way to do so, is to behave in ways that make
the Beast want to return, but there isn't a guarantee that it will wish to do so.
The only other way is for the vampire to kill the host, but such an act requires
the sacrifice of permenant humanity, or path of enlightenment ratings, and as
normal, this degeneration, opens the possibility that the vampire will gain a 
permenant derangement. 
           
{WHack and Slash / Out of Character{x
      
The target of this power instantly frenzies.
       
{WSyntax:{x  draw {W<target>{x
       
{RCurrently this power only works on other players and does not affect NPC's.{x
       
~
1 share~

{RSharing of Spirits{x
     {RAnimalism{x {W****{x
                  
By locking his gaze with that of a beast, the vampire may psychically possess 
the animal.  Some older Kindred believe that since animals have no souls, and 
only spirits, the kindred can move his own soul into the animal's body. Most
younger Canites think it is a matter of transferring one's consciousness into
the animal's mind.  In either case, it is agreed that the beast's weaker mind
is pushed aside by the Kindred's own consciousness.  The vampire's body falls
into a motionless state akin to torpor while his mind takes control of the 
animal's actions, remaining this way until the Kindred's consciousness is 
returned.  Tzimisce seldom use this power, considering it debasing to enter the
body of a lesser creature, and when they do stoop to using it, they possess only
predators.  Conversely, Gangrel revel in connecting to the natural world in this 
fundamental way, taking delight in sampling different animal natures.
                  
  
{WTable Top / Role Play{x
          
The vampire loos into the animal's eyes, and rolls the following:
          
{WSystem:{x Charisma + Animal Ken {WDiff:{x {R8{x
           
The number of successes obtained determines how thorough the character over-rides
the animal's spirit.  Fewer than {R3{x successes means the character must use
Temporary Willpower Points{D[{WTWP{D]{x, to take any action that directly violates
the instincts of the animal in question.  With fewer than {R5{x successes the
possessing character behaves much like the animal.  His soul becomes clouded with
needs and impulses from the animal's spirit and body.  Multiple successes allow the
Canite to utilize some mental Disciplines while possessing the animal.
    
{R1{x    Success   -   {WNone{x 
{R2{x  Successes   -   {WAuspex{x
{R3{x  Successes   -   {WPresence{x
{R4{x  Successes   -   {WDementation{x {D|{x {WDominate{x
{R5+{x Successes   -   {WChimerstry{x {D|{x {WThaumaturgy{x {D| {WMortis{x
    
        
This power entwines the Canite's consciousness closely with the animal's 
spirit, so much so, that the vampire may continue to think and feel like that 
animal even after breaking the connection.  This effect continues until the 
character spends a total of {R7{x Willpower Points to resist and finally 
overcome the bestial nature.  This should be role-played, although to a
lessening degree as Willpower is spent.
       
If the vampire experienced anything traumatic, that spooked or caused the
animal to frenzy, the following must be rolled when the Canite severs the
bond, with failure causing further complications.
         
{WSystem:{x Wits + Empathy {WDiff:{x {R8{x
      
Failure indicates that the kindred's mind returns to his own body, but still 
thinks in purely animalistic terms.  A botch sends the character into frenzy 
upon return.    
      
The Canite may travel as far from his own body as he is physically able while
possessing the animal, but retains no conscious connection with his body.
The vampire may also venture out during the day, while merged with the beast,
as long as, he meets the requiremnts, and makes a successful roll to remain
awake.  The kindred may dismiss the sharing at anytime, and his conciousness
returns to his physical form instantly.
         
Although the vampire has no conscious link to his body while possessing the 
animal, he does form a sympathetic bond.  Anything the animal feels the Canite 
also experiences.  In fact, any damage the animal's body sustains is also 
applied to the character's body, although the Kindred may Soak if he is able.
If the animal dies before the vampire's soul can flee from the body, the 
character's body falls into torpor.  Presumably, this is in sympathetic 
response to the massive trauma of death, although some Kindred believe that
the vampire's soul is cast adrift, and must find its way back to his body.
          
{WHack and Slash / Our of Character{x
     
{RThis power is currently disabled, but will do something neato in the future.{x
~
1 songofserenity~

{RSong of Serenity{x
     {RAnimalism{x {W***{x
     
As the supreme predators of the natural world, Kindred are highly attuned to 
the bestial nature that dwells within every mortal heart.  A vampire who 
develops this power may assert his will over a mortal, human or animal, 
subject, subduing the Beast within her.  This quenches all powerful and 
assertive emotions, within the target.  The Kindred must either touch his
subject or stare into her eyes to channel his will effectively.
          
Mortals who lack the fire of their inner Beasts are quite tractable, reacting 
to even stressful situations with indifference.  Even the most courageous or 
maddened mortal becomes apathetic and listless, while an especially sensitive 
individual may suffer from a phobic derangement while affected by this power. 
        
      
{WTable Top / Role Play{x
             
This power can be invoked in two different manners, although the effect is the
same either way.  The kindred can either scare the beast away using the first
roll, or sooth it into complacency using the second.  
       
{WSystem:{x Manipulation + Intimidation {WDiff:{x {R7{x (Fear)       
{WSystem:{x Manipulation + Empathy {WDiff:{x {R7{x (Sooth) 
                
This is an extended action requiring as many total successes as the target has
Current Willpower.  Failure indicates that the player must start over from the
beginning, while a botch indicates that the vampire may never again affect that
subject's Beast.
       
When a mortal's Beast is cowed or soothed, she can no longer use or regain 
Willpower.  She ceases all struggles, whether mental or physical. She doesn't 
even defend herself if assaulted, though may roll Willpower if her life is 
threatened.  To recover from this power, the mortal rolls:
   
{WSystem:{x Willpower {WDiff: {R6{x 
This is done once per day until she accumulates enough successes equal to the
vampire's own Willpower.   Finally,  other vampires cannot be affected by 
this power.      
 
{WHack and Slash / Out of Character{x
{WSyntax:{c  song <target>{x
Song of Serenity removes the aggro flag from mobiles.  This will not remove 
a mob that has a grudge against you.   
    
~
1 sweetwhispers feralspeech~

{RFeral Speech{x
{R        Animalism {W*{x

This power is the basis from which all other Animalism abilities grow.  The 
vampire creates an empathic connection with a beast, thereby allowing him to 
communicate or issue simple commands. The Kindred locks eyes with the animal, 
transmitting his desires through sheer force of will.  Although it isn't 
necessary to actually "speak" in chirps, hisses or barks, some vampires find 
that doing so helps strengthen the connection with the animal.  Eye contact 
must be maintained the entire time; if it's broken, the Kindred must look into 
the beast's eyes once again to regain contact. 
Since Sweet Whispers requires eye contact, animal that cannot see are not 
affected.  Further, the simpler the creature, the more difficult it becomes to 
connect with the animal's Beast.  Mammals, predatory birds and larger reptiles 
are relatively easy to communicate with. Insects, invertebrates and most fish 
(with the possible exception of larger ones like sharks) are just too simple, 
their Beasts too weak, to connect with. 
Sweet Whispers provides no guarantees that an animal will want to deal with the 
vampire, nor does it ensure that the animal will pursue any requests the 
vampire makes of it.  Still, it does at least make the creature better disposed 
toward the Kindred.  The manner in which the vampire presents his desires to 
the animal often depends on the type of creature.  A Kindred can probably cow 
smaller beasts into heeding commands, but he's better off couching orders for 
large predators in terms of requests. 
If the vampire successfully uses the power, the animal performs the command to
the best of its ability and intellect.  Only the very brightest creatures 
understand truly complex directives (orders dealing with conditional
situations or requiring abstract logic).  Commands that the animal does
understand remain deeply implanted, however, and may affect it for some
time. 

{WTable Top / Role Play{x
{WSystem:{x No roll is necessary to talk with an animal, but the character must 
establish eye contact first.  Issuing commands requires a Manipulation + Animal 
Ken roll.  The difficulty depends on the creature: Predatory mammals (wolves, 
cats, insectivorous/vampire bats) are difficulty 6, other mammals and predatory 
birds (rats, owls) are difficulty 7, other birds and reptiles (hawks, snakes) 
are difficulty 8.  This difficulty is reduced by one if the character speaks to 
the animal in its "native tongue" and can be adjusted further by circumstances 
and role-playing skill (we highly recommend that all communication between 
characters and animals be role-played). 

The number of successes the player achieves dictates how strongly the 
character's command affects the animal.  One success is sufficient to have a 
cat follow someone and lead the character to the same location, three successes 
are enough to have a raven spy on a target for weeks, and five successes ensure 
that a grizzly ferociously guards the entrance to the character's wilderness 
have for some months.  

The character's Nature plays a large part in how he approaches these 
conversations. The character might try intimidation, teasing, cajoling,
rationality, or emotional pleading.  The player should understand that he does 
not simply play his character in these situations, but the Beast Within as well.

{WOut of Character / Hack and Slash{x
{WSyntax: {cferal <target> <command>{x
This power costs blood to activate, and causes an animal to comply with a one-
word command.  Only natural animals of above average intelligence can be
affected.

{R*{xAdditionally, by simply having Animalism 1, a player can converse with
{x talkative animals [{W!{x] by using the {ctalk{x command.  Each use of Feral Speech
{x in this manner will use a small portion of blood.

See Also:  {WAnimalism Gangrel Nosferatu Tzimisce{x
~
1 beckoning~

{RBeckoning{x
{R        Animalism {D**{x
 
The vampire's connection to the Beast grows strong enough that he may call out 
in the voice of a specific type of animal - howling like a wolf, cawing like a 
raven, etc. This call mystically summons creatures of the chosen type. Since 
each type of animal has a different call, Beckoning works on only a single 
species at a time.
 
All such animals within earshot are summoned, but may choose individually 
whether or not to respond. While the vampire has no control over the beasts 
who answer, the animals who do are favorably disposed toward him and are at 
least willing to listen to the Kindred's request.
 
{WTable Top / Role Play{x
{WSystem:{x The player rolls his Charisma+Survival (difficulty 6) to determine the 
response to the character's call; consult the table below. Only animals that 
can hear the cry will respond. If the Storyteller decides no animals of that 
type are within earshot, the summons goes unanswered. 
 
The call can be as specific as the player desires. A character could call for 
all bats in the area, for only the male bats nearby, or for only the albino bat 
with the notched ear he saw the other night. 
 
1 success A single animal responds. 
2 successes One-quarter of the animals within  earshot respond. 
3 successes Half of the animals respond. 
4 successes Most of the  animals respond. 
5 successes All of the animals respond. 
 
{WHack and Slash / Out of Character{x
{WSyntax:  {cbeckoning{x
This ability calls a charmed pet rat(s) to fight with you.  It costs blood
to summon the rats.

See also:  Animalism, Gangrel, Nosferatu, Tzimisce
 
{rNote{x:  Only one charmed mob is attached to your character at a time.  
IE: One set of allies, one set of rats, one shadow spirit, etc...
~
