0 backgrounds do_backgrounds~

BACKGROUNDS
 
These Traits describe advantages of birth (or rebirth), circumstance and 
opportunity: material possessions, social networks and the like. Backgrounds 
are external, not internal, Traits, and you should always rationalize how you 
came to possess them, as well as what they represent. Who are your contacts? 
Why do your allies support you? Where did you meet your retainers? How exactly 
do you make enough money to justify your four dots in Resources? If you've put 
enough detail into your character concept, selecting appropriate Backgrounds 
should be easy.
 
Although it's uncommon to make rolls involving Background Traits, your 
Storyteller might have you do so to see if you can obtain information, goods or 
favors. For example, you might have to roll Wits + Resources to keep your 
investments healthy, or Manipulation + Contacts to wheedle that extra favor 
from your smuggler "associate."
 
{WSyntax:{c  background{x

See also:  Allies, Generation, Mentor, Status, Contacts, Herd, Resources, 
Rituals, Fame, Influence, Retainers
~
-1 VIRTUES~

VIRTUES ;
Virtues refers to the conscience, self-control and courage of a character.
They are the humanistic side of the Vampire and help determine the emotional
reactions of the Vampire.  Each player starts with one dot in each of the three
virtues.

     * Conscience
       Conscience is what determines if a character feels any remorse for their
       actions.  Humanity loss is based on one's conscience.
     
     * Self-control
       Self-control is what determines if a Vampire resists the urge or gives
       in and goes into a frenzy, which can be based on many different factors
       one of which is loss of blood or hunger.
     
     * Courage
       Courage is what determines if a character can stand in the face of
       danger.  Will they run at the threat of open flames or the sun.

Also help on: ADVANTAGES BACKGROUNDS HEALTH HUMANITY WILLPOWER
~
-1 HEALTH~

HEALTH :
Health is determined on a seven level system.  At this time, it is not fully 
implemented.

Also help on: ADVANTAGES BACKGROUNDS VIRTUES HUMANITY WILLPOWER
~
-1 HUMANITY~

HUMANITY :
Humanity is critical for determining how far a character has degenerated into
beastiality.  A character who has no Humanity has totally succumbed to the
Beast and is no longer in control of their own actions.

Starting Humanity is derived by adding ones Conscience and Self-control.

Also help on: ADVANTAGES BACKGROUNDS VIRTUES HEALTH WILLPOWER
~
0 willpower~

A character's Willpower is the representation of her dedication and
sheer inner strength.  During times of stress or adversity, often one
is required to dig deep into the physical and mental reserves, forcing
oneself to overcome or resist situations that go against your very
nature.  Vampires often spend Willpower to temporarily resist their
predatory natures, fighting against the Beast.  A variety of powers,
ranging from Disciplines to skills, cost Willpower points to activate.

Your character's starting Willpower is equal to your starting Courage
rating.  Willpower must be raised with Freebie points, and raising
Courage after creation does not increase your Willpower.  They are
raised separately.
 
You can see your Willpower rating on the {ccs2{x sheet.  Two ratings are
listed underneath.  The first is Permanent and the second is Temporary.
Permanent Willpower is usually used for when you roll Willpower.
Temporary Willpower is spent as a cost for some abilities.

Every in-game morning at 6AM, Humans/Mages/Werewolves regain a
point of Temporary Willpower.  Vampires regain a point at 6PM.
Additionally, killing a mob of your level or higher gives a small
chance to regain a point of Willpower.

{WSee Also:  Advantages Backgrounds Virtues Health Humanity{x
~
-1 RENOWN~

RENOWN - GLORY/HONOR/WISDOM
 
Renown is a system that determines how well a character is living up to his 
expected role in Garou society. It is for this reason that Renown is connected 
so closely with auspices. Renown differs from the experience point system 
because it involves much more roleplaying. A character could rack up plenty of 
experience for his actions, but if his deeds conflict with his auspice duties, 
his Renown will not increase.
 
Characters with low Renown, such as starting characters, are expected to 
respect and defer to werewolves of higher rank. Garou who ignore this system 
find themselves looked down upon, and they lose more Renown as a result.
 
Renown is measure in three different areas: Glory, Honor and Wisdom. Each one 
is somewhat self-explanatory. Glory represents the physical deeds of the Garou: 
feats of strength, stamina and agility, such as those that made Hercules a 
legend. It also measures bravery, extreme risk and victories in battle. Honor 
measures a Garou's sense of duty and history. It speaks of the character's 
ethics and morals, as well as his personal sense of pride. And finally, Wisdom 
celebrates the more mental virtues of the character. It includes strategy, 
cunning and insight. Patience and a strong connection to the spirit world also 
help a Garou increased his Wisdom.
 
One paper, Renown takes two forms. The dots represent the character's Permanent 
Renown rating while the squares denote the Renown pool and show many temporary 
points the character has earned. Renown differs from Gnosis and Willpower in 
that the Renown pool is allowed to exceed the Renown rating. Permanent Renown 
changes very little, and it changes only in dramatic circumstances. Temporary 
Renown is always in flux, and it might shift several times over one session.
 
When a Garou gains enough Renown, she increases in rank.

(OOC): Garou gain points in temporary renown randomly each remort. This is to
allow H&S players to gain what would normally be a wholly IC stat. Random
renown gain simulates in part the trials and challenges you face during a remort.
If all three of your temporary renown traits are full, you will not be able
to remort and you will receive a notice. This notice includes directions on
how to override the block if you wish to remort and not receive any renown.

To change your temporary renown to permanent renown type 'freebie' to see 
the cost and then 'freebie wisdom/glory/honor' when you have 10 temporary 
renown to convert it to one point of permenant renown.

See also:  Garou, Rank, Honor, Glory, Wisdom
~
0 form breed shapeshift~

Form:  The shape in which a Garou appears at the current moment.
Breed:  The form in which a Garou was born.

           {wForms                    Breeds{x
           Homid		        Homid
           Glabro		        Metis
           Crinos		        Lupus
           Hispo
{x           Lupus

For further help on Form or Breed, type "help (specific Form/Breed)".

{WSyntax to change forms: {cshapeshift <form>{x

Also help on: GAROU TRIBES AUSPICE GIFTS
~
-1 HOMID~

HOMID :
Homid refers to both the form and breed of a Garou.

A Garou whose breed is Homid, is born looking like that of a normal human
child.  A Homid child most often is born to parents who are also Homid, but
it has been known. on the rare occasion, for a homid to be born to a Lupus.

A Garou who is in the Homid form is presenting himself to others in a way so
as to appear as a normal human.  In this form they can blend into any city.


Also help on: GAROU TRIBES AUSPICE GIFTS BREED FORM RAGE RENOWN CRINOS METIS
LUPUS
~
-1 CRINOS METIS~

CRINOS METIS :
Crinos refers to the form of the Garou in his most terrifying state.  The
Crinos form is said to cause delirium in Humans.  They tower seven plus feet 
tall and weigh nearly two thousand pounds.  They are meant for battle and are 
simply put ... giant killing machines.

Metis refers to the breed of the Garou, the form in which they were born.
Their natural form is Crinos, and although they can hide this form by shifting 
to another one, should they fall unconcious, they revert back to their natural 
state and run the risk of causing the delirium in humans. These creatures are 
all have physical deformities of one sort of another and are shunned by Garou 
society as their birth is the result of the mating of two Garou, which is a 
crime against the Litany, or Garou Law.  These Garou are sterile, which means 
they can not have children and thus effectively end their family line.  In most 
tribes, these Metis children are either spirited away to live in secret places 
until they come of age and are able to live on their own.  Or, in most cases, 
are killed at birth along with the parents.

Also help on: GAROU TRIBES AUSPICE GIFTS BREED FORM RAGE RENOWN HOMID LUPUS
~
-1 LUPUS~

LUPUS :
Lupus refers to both the form and breed of a Garou.

A Garou whose breed is Lupus is born looking like that of a normal wolf pup.
Like their homid counterparts, Lupus generally give birth to Lupus, but it is
possible for a Homid to birth a Lupus.

A Garou who is in the Lupus form, has assumed the look of that of a wolf.  It
is in this form that the Garou can travel over longer distances at a quicker
pace, and can hunt more effectively than a human.


Also help on: GAROU TRIBES AUSPICE GIFTS BREED FORM RAGE RENOWN HOMID CRINOS
METIS
~
-1 AUSPICE~

AUSPICE :
Auspice refes to the phase of the moon under which a Garou is born.  Each phase
affects the personality of the pup and grants them a different gift than her
brothers and sisters.  Regardless of their breed, all Garou feel an inexorable
pull toward Luna, sister of Gaia.  Whether she shines on them with her full
face or hides from view, they take comfort from her company and guidance.  Luna
is the one who shows a Garou what his path and role will be in Garou society,
and this path is called an auspice.

Haven allows for a player to chose their own Auspice.

	{wMoon Phase 		Auspice{x
	New Moon		    Ragabash
	Crescent Moon		Theurge
	Half Moon		    Philodox
	Gibbous Moon		Galliard
	Full Moon		    Ahroun

For further help on Auspices, type "help (specific Auspice)".

Also help on: GAROU TRIBES GIFTS BREED FORM
~
-1 RAGABASH~

RAGABASH :
Only a real fool ignores the wisdom hiding in the guise of the trickster. She
brings mirth to still the anger between two hearts and humility to those who
need a dose in full.  Moreover, the New Moon often has clever insights that
make her a worthy advisor (or conniver).  The Ragabash occupies an odd place in
werewolf society.  Many distrust her unpredictable mannerisms and peculiar
sense of humor, but she's usually welcomed and given a free hand in the
day-to-day life of the sept.  The New Moon enjoys a rare flexibility within the
normally rigid strictures of the werewolf society.  When there's tension in the
air, the Ragabash is usually the one to lift it, even at the risk of a claw
raking across her exposed, laughing throat.  She knows a different kind of
courage than the warrior, but one that clever elders don't underestimate.  As
the "questioner of the ways," the Ragabash has an obligation to play devil's
advocate.  Being a New Moon is more than being given a license to play pranks
or undercut authority -- it is a real responsibility.  The Ragabash must
question traditions and decrees not reflexivly, but rather from an informed
point of view.  It isn't enough to cast doubt on an elder's proclamation; the
Ragabash must have a solid argument supporting her contrary views.  It's her 
ask to make certain that her packmates see both sides to every issue, to be
sure that they're taking the right option, not just the most obvious one.  Of
course, the Ragabash's penchant for cunning, sly tactics and subtle Gifts also
tends to bestow a certain amount of responsiblity as a scout, sabotuer or even
assassin.  The new moon is the moon of stealth, and its children are the ones
charged with using that stealth for the Garou's benefit. 

Also help on: GAROU TRIBES AUSPICE GIFTS BREED FORM RAGE RENOWN
~
-1 THEURGE~

THEURGE ;
As Luna's most slender light shines on her sister Gaia, she reveals secrets of
the spirits and their vast realms.  The Theurge is the child of the crescent
moon, and he is wisest in the ways of the Umbra and its inhabitants.  Some call
these seers the daydreamers of the werewolves and many do seem to be detached
from their brethren.  Theurge hold an important place in any pack for the Garou
would forget the spiritual side of their nature.  The Theurge is usually the
pack's ritemaster and takes the responsibility of performing them for the pack
and is the one who typically speaks the language of the spirits and assists the
Garou in receiving gifts from Gaia via the spirits.  The Theurge is the one who
calls forth the spirit from the sacred Caern in hopes that the spirit will
bestow a gift upon a chosen Garou.

Also help on: GAROU TRIBES AUSPICE GIFTS BREED FORM RAGE RENOWN
~
-1 PHILODOX~

PHILODOX :
The half moon reflects the dualities of Garou natures:  wolf and human, flesh
and spirit, fury and wisdom, darkness and light.  The Philodox is the lawkeeper
of the pack.  The Philodox is born with the wisdom and desire to judge fairly
the actions of her people.  She can't help but to try to solve every dispute
and is usually the leader of the pack.  A good Philodox is concerned with the
honor of each and every werewolf she meets.  It is easy for the half moons to
become disillusioned at times and should be the first one to warn any Garou on
the rule of the litany.  She's expected to remain impartial when it comes to
her packmates.

Also help on: GAROU TRIBES AUSPICE GIFTS BREED FORM RAGE RENOWN
~
-1 GALLIARD~

GALLIARD :
The Galliard, beloved of the nigh-full moon, is joy incarnate in his songs,
stories and poetry.  The beauty of Luna's gibbous face inspires him to great
deeds on the battlefield as well as around the moot fires.  He keeps the
traditions of the werewolves alive through lore-carried from generation to
generation.  A Galliard can rouse a pack from self-pity and suffering when
their claws are needed for battle and he can cause even the biggest to weep
when the need arises.  He might be a dancer, a storyteller or a musician.  When
peace turns to war, the Moon Dancer may well lead the pack and when the
tranquility comes back he will sing laments for the fallen and epic sagas for
the brave survivors.

A Galliard is the lore-keeper of the pack and is very well versed in the Garou
history and is able to teach others to learn from past mistakes.  He is also
the one who recounts his packmates' deeds of bravery, wisdom and honor at moots
ensuring that they will be properly honored.  The Galliard is also the one who
negotiates with mortals and others.  The Galliard has a fine memory, a glib
tongue and a brave spirit.

Also help on: GAROU TRIBES AUSPICE GIFTS BREED FORM RAGE RENOWN
~
-1 AHROUN~

AHROUN :
The Warrior basks in the full glow of Luna, the silver light illumination his
all-consuming rage.  He doesn't hesitate to strike fatal blows; rather, he 
often doesn't know when to stop his killing.  All werewolves are fighters but
the Ahroun is the most destructive and vicious of all of Gaia's children.  The
full moon knows no cowardice, only his thirst for battle. When peace falters,
he takes the reins of the command as is his due, being the first to lead the
pack into the fray and the last to fall should they meet defeat.  The Ahroun's
role in the pack is simple, he must be the consummate warrior, able to
physically defend hit weaker packmates as well as provide the right tactic for
any circumstance.

Also help on: GAROU TRIBES AUSPICE GIFTS BREED FORM RAGE RENOWN
~
-1 TRIBES~

TRIBES :
The tribes of the Garou are as diverse as the Vampire clans.  Each has their
own gift that Gaia has granted them.  Just like the Kindred, Garou are social
creatures and tend to stay close to the members of their tribe.  While there is
not the infighting in Garou society like there is among the Kindred, each Tribe
does see theirs as superior to the others.

Tribes Available on Haven:
Black_Furies, Bone_Gnawers, Children_of_Gaia, Get_of_Fenris, Fianna, 
Red_Talons, Shadow_Lords, Silent_Striders, Silver_Fangs, Warders_of_Men

For further help on Tribes, type "help (specific Tribe)".

Also help on: GAROU AUSPICE GIFTS BREED FORM RAGE RENOWN
~
0 clans~

{D              "{rThere is strength in the Blood.{D"{x
Mortals are social creatures, and as Vampires were once mortal they too have
retained this part of their mortal life: the need to be with others of their
own kind. The Vampire race is divided into groups called clans.  Each clan
has their own strengths and weaknesses.  While one clan may have disciplines
which lend to a combat oriented role in society, others are the diplomats,
and their disciplines reflect such.  Some clans are of the social elite while
others do well just to eek out a nightly existance.  The one major thing is
that a clan is your family, and a family should get along and fight for one
another no matter what.  It is Brujah vs. Ventrue, Toreador vs. Nosferatu,
Assamite vs. Tremere.  Let us just hope that you know which side you stand on,
or you might find one of your elders standing over you with a stake.

{chelp <clan>{x

